details.body:
## Problem

`getExtensionGalleryManifestFromServiceUrl` only guards against a null body:

```ts
const extensionGalleryManifest = await asJson<IExtensionGalleryManifest>(context);
if (!extensionGalleryManifest) {
    throw new Error('Unable to retrieve extension gallery manifest.');
}
return extensionGalleryManifest;
```

`asJson` throws on non-2xx, so error statuses are handled. But **any 200 carrying any JSON is accepted as a service index.** A body of `{"foo":"bar"}` is non-null, so it is returned, `update(manifest)` publishes it, and the status becomes `Available`. The user sees a working marketplace backed by a garbage manifest; endpoint discovery then throws later, outside this `try/catch`.

Realistic trigger: a captive portal / corporate proxy / SSO interstitial that answers `200` with an HTML-ish or JSON error payload instead of the service index.

## Fix

Validate the shape before returning. Throwing routes into the existing `catch`, which already maps to `AccessDenied` — so no new status and no new error type:

```ts
if (!Array.isArray(extensionGalleryManifest.resources)
    || !extensionGalleryManifest.resources.every(resource => resource && typeof resource.id === 'string' && typeof resource.type === 'string')) {
    throw new Error('Service index response is not a valid extension gallery manifest.');
}
```

## Tests

Adds `src/vs/workbench/services/extensionManagement/test/electron-browser/extensionGalleryManifestService.test.ts` (new folder) with the shared harness plus two regressions:

1. Eligible account, **200 with a non-manifest body** (`{ foo: 'bar' }`) → must be `AccessDenied`, not `Available`.
2. Eligible account, **200 with malformed resources** (`{ version: '1.0', resources: [{}] }`) → same.

Verified before/after by stashing only the source fix (test left in place):

- **Before the fix:** both new tests fail with `actual 'available'` vs `expected 'accessDenied'` (the four baseline cases pass).
- **After the fix:** all cases pass.

## Note

This is independent of any other work on this file. If it happens to land alongside another change touching the same method, a merge conflict there is expected and trivial to resolve.

